### OK to change the page router so in-app links can carry an absolute `href`: same-origin links navigate in place, other-app links open normally?

**Background.** `href` is always absolute (`https://who.heliosian.com/people/…`). Every client link made by `link()` (`web/public/common/elements.js`) is marked `data-link`, and the router (`web/common/router.js`) catches clicks on those and calls `history.pushState(…, a.getAttribute('href'))`. That works for `/people/x`, but for a link to another app (`https://team.heliosian.com/v/…`) `pushState` throws and the click does nothing, so today `railLink` in `web/common/rail.js` strips `data-link` from absolute links as a special case, and other cross-app links avoid `link()`. The router's back trail also compares paths, so an absolute same-app href would not match.

**Example, a Who? directory card linking `href` https://who.heliosian.com/people/perWPWxQkmB98q, and Who?'s rail linking https://team.heliosian.com/v/grp4xuQMKn2N0b:**

- (*) Router handles origins
  The click handler reads the link's resolved URL: same origin → `navigate(pathname + search)` (Who? card: in-place, `/people/perWPWxQkmB98q` in the trail); other origin → it returns and the browser follows (Team link loads Team). About three changed lines in `router.js`; `railLink` is deleted and every page uses `link(row.href)` as is.
- ( ) Strip origins in each page
  Each same-app use cuts the origin off (`new URL(row.href).pathname`) before `link()`, and each cross-app use builds a plain `<a>` without `data-link`. No router change, but every call site has to know whether its row lives on this app.

